WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.248
Module eight puts everything together.

2
00:00:02.248 --> 00:00:07.054
We connect a twin controller to SUMO, give it a camera, and measure what it achieves.

3
00:00:07.954 --> 00:00:17.113
Every simulated second the twin senses the road, estimates queues and arriving vehicles, decides whether to keep or end each green, and acts through TraCI.

4
00:00:17.113 --> 00:00:19.772
Then SUMO moves the traffic forward one step.

5
00:00:20.672 --> 00:00:22.859
Here is the corridor at medium demand.

6
00:00:22.859 --> 00:00:24.989
Dots are vehicles, coloured by speed.

7
00:00:24.989 --> 00:00:28.385
The orange circles are the cameras' range at each junction.

8
00:00:28.385 --> 00:00:33.278
Watch the queues on the side streets form and clear as the twin switches the signals.

9
00:00:34.178 --> 00:00:45.890
The virtual camera has the flaws of real computer vision: limited range, missed detections that grow with distance, cars hidden behind other cars, position noise, and guessing which lane a car is in.

10
00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:56.435
On five seeds the twin never saw, both twins beat SUMO's actuated signals at high demand, about ninety-five seconds of lost time per trip against one hundred and one.

11
00:00:56.435 --> 00:01:00.096
At low and medium demand, actuated control was slightly better.

12
00:01:00.996 --> 00:01:03.144
The lesson is to measure, not assume.

13
00:01:03.144 --> 00:01:07.613
The twin always reduced stops, but cut delay only when the corridor was busy.

14
00:01:07.613 --> 00:01:11.966
Camera detection fell from eighty-four to fifty-six percent as queues grew.

15
00:01:11.966 --> 00:01:14.578
Both sensing and decision logic limit a twin.
